David Damerell wrote:

> Ranged combat does more damage in many cases and does not
> expose you to most flavours of counterattack. What's not
> to like?

The fact that it uses vital resources.

I think we all agree that in certain cases, ranged attack is
essential---you don't want to let the monster come close.
If you have limited resources for ranged attack (a limited
number of daggers, limited manna), then it is probably a
poor strategy to use them when not necessary.

I think the key here is the idea of resources, and that hit
points are also a resource.  If you're a Valk, dual
weaponning Excalibur and a long sword, with 15 daggers
in your quiver, decent armor and something like 150 hit
points, it's better to mel=E9e that mumak, so that the daggers
are still in your quiver if a mind flayer shows up
immediately after your fight (before you've had time to pick
up the daggers).  If you're a ranger whose run into a lot of
arrow traps early on, and you've got arrows to spare, the
calcualation is different.  In every case, when you have a
choice, you look at what you've got, and what you can most
afford to spare.  Given the way the fighter classes level
up, hit points are often what you can most afford to spare,
and if that's all that you risk in mel=E9e combat, then mel=E9e
combat is the way to go.

(Obviously, you want to consider damage as well.  But for
most monsters, the choice between dual weaponing Excalibur
and a long sword, and throwing daggers at expert, isn't
significant.  Both will do the job more than adequately.)

